{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-112506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-1I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-112506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x$  only"] ["text" "$x$ and  $z$  only"] ["text" "$y$  only"] ["text" "$y$ and  $z$  only"] ["text" "$z$  only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y$  only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-2).$  \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)^{2}x=(A+2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-112500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x$ and $y$ only"] ["text" "$x$ and $z$ only"] ["text" "$x$ only"] ["text" "$y$ only"] ["text" "$z$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-1)I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-112505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x$ and $y$ only"] ["text" "$x$ only"] ["text" "$y$ only"] ["text" "$z$ and $x$ only"] ["text" "$z$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$z$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(0).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(0) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-0I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-112432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x$ and $z$ only"] ["text" "$x$ only"] ["text" "$y$ and $z$ only"] ["text" "$y$ only"] ["text" "$z$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(2).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}y=(A-2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-112497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x$ and $z$ only"] ["text" "$x$ only"] ["text" "$y$ and $z$ only"] ["text" "$y$ only"] ["text" "$z$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-112497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109364" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109364" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109364" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{-2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=3-2\\cdot 1=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $2a+2b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a+2b=2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2\\cdot 1=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-108728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-108728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=1-2\\cdot 1=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-108728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-108728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-108728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-108728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=\\frac{5}{2}-2\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $6a-3b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $6a-3b=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-3\\cdot 2=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "860d28956a2f0266819717825073a8a7b609413dd38ff4339138db5e452b375b"} {"question" "q-109377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "q-109377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/state.json" "file_sha256" "88c3c803dfcab7d3fc94218f91574fc54f7752c1c0b8b70cfb43ee18f6b8184e"} {"question" "e-9227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/54ae1bc6614a1fe70fac039fbdd3defd2aeeceb3648679ee3d4c9ad10f6f15b5.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/a985e5861e7360654b98ca334fac6d55f435e271dcc52b313e350f74a4c755c5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/example-9227.json" "file_sha256" "836e138f7c3d6b97fb8b53ecb637c7f42f7f2da8dbbd4fea12d33b62be41bb91"} {"question" "e-9227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J = J_2(1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda=1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $\\mathbf x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/624e955f479c8193ff32f3bebf0decb7b1d6b45fb5ec44db637d956e3a8d1ea7.png)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/0855b40d83950bcdfeda8447e74187f498fbfe73050e1e8fd7755a1ad4b1dbd0.png)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/example-9227.json" "file_sha256" "836e138f7c3d6b97fb8b53ecb637c7f42f7f2da8dbbd4fea12d33b62be41bb91"} {"question" "e-9088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/8a5b765f60afdb05946a2ed393c2776c532fca4c2905969928b8dc66b55d8913.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/example-9088.json" "file_sha256" "6ae3935ed0e04a673f10e504b385b0285a0d2f02c40f8f8b13103be34c344863"} {"question" "e-9088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/ca51c47bc0e065fa8ec1303f235f5b5e0c818bf271abbfb90ba96219fd6dd91a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/34e1d2e3dcdfd3c46c3e2ca5360fd596e307efe042f6676413736a100d058155.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-1}{1} = -1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/example-9088.json" "file_sha256" "6ae3935ed0e04a673f10e504b385b0285a0d2f02c40f8f8b13103be34c344863"} {"question" "e-9089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/35e38dfcccab52240fa316b41acacf74548e7218a5d79d856f69c947f3d26394.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/example-9089.json" "file_sha256" "a49a215909e00c95b6599a1fe28a9a234ecd014ea1be413f7345cc0b82675e5e"} {"question" "e-9089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/9bff79e13c06645253e57b8e6f43689c7ce21ac818c3ffdc677e03b5a9b08eb7.png) of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ such that $(A-4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A - 4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-\\dfrac 15 - x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/44f1c49ffc09942f7062c8e4da4419a214551cdf659ee92be03556c52a2f3005.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=0,$ we get  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/assets/e2ad676d41fff87d0c519bf6cbb369a5ec9ac209c8b2b9353a658d9307d6109d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070862/example-9089.json" "file_sha256" "a49a215909e00c95b6599a1fe28a9a234ecd014ea1be413f7345cc0b82675e5e"}] "usage" {"e-9088" [] "q-112432" [] "q-109381" [] "e-9089" [] "q-109368" [] "q-108728" [] "e-9227" []} "basis" 2512 "retractions" [[17592186176202 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, z = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186176156 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$."] [17592186176157 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $a - 2b$."] [17592186176148 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $a - 2b$."] [17592186175785 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186175785 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J = J_2(1)$.\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\nJ_{2}(1) \\mid: \\mid v_{2}→^(A - 1 I)v_{1}→^(A - 1 I)0\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda=1$.\n\nNow, computing $A-1I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 1I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $\\mathbf x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$, since\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}→^(A - 1 I)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}→^(A - 1 I)0\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."] [17592186175762 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$."] [17592186175762 :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$.\n\nNow, computing $A-4I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 4I &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} - (4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-x_2$. Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-1}{1} = -1\n$$"] [17592186175763 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the second column of the matrix $P$."] [17592186175763 :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that\n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$,\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ such that $(A-4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1$.\n\nNow, computing $A-4I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 4I &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - (4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - 4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac 15 - x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5} - x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173551 17592186173552 17592186173553 17592186173554 17592186173555 17592186173599 17592186173600 17592186173601 17592186173602 17592186173603 17592186173605 17592186173606 17592186173607 17592186173608 17592186173609 17592186173875 17592186173876 17592186173877 17592186173878 17592186173879] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173550 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173550 :answer-fiel